 	<TEXT>
 		<Tag>TXT_KEY_CONCEPT_ESPIONAGE_PEDIA_SR</Tag>
-		<English>[H1]Espionage[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Espionage Points[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Using the Espionage Slider, players may assign a percentage of their trade income towards the production of Espionage Points. On the Espionage Screen, these points may be assigned to leaders that the player has met. These points may be used to perform various Active and Passive Espionage Missions. Active Missions require the player to spend Espionage Points and have a spy in the territory of the opponent they wish to perform the mission against. Passive Missions will take effect as the player accrues more points against a specific opponent. Having more espionage points accumulated will increase the cost of your opponents' missions towards you and reduce the cost of your missions towards them.[PARAGRAPH:1]The [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]Courthouse[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Jail[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_PALACE]Palace[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_CASTLE]Castle[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]Security Bureau[\LINK] and [LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]Intelligence Agency[\LINK] all increase the production of Espionage points. The [LINK=CIVIC_NATIONHOOD]Nationhood[\LINK] Civic also provides an Espionage bonus and the Security Bureau Building increases the point cost of enemy missions in your cities.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Espionage Screen[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Espionage Points may be assigned to specific opponents on the Espionage Screen. If you do not assign points to specific leaders, they will be evenly divided between all known civs. By increasing the "Weight" item next to each opponent's portrait, you can assign the portion you would like each opponent to receive. Increasing the weight next to an opponent's name to "1" will assign all Espionage Points to that opponent. Increasing one opponent's weight to "2" while keeping two other opponents' weight at "1" and another's at "0" will give the first opponent twice as many points as the second and third, while the fourth will receive none. You may assign Weights in any fractions you like, up to 99. In this way, you can finely tune the portion of Espionage Points you would like assigned to each of your opponents.[PARAGRAPH:1]Below "Weight" is a number that shows the cost modifier for your Espionage Missions against this opponent. This number is based on the total number of Espionage Points you have spent throughout the game against this opponent. If the number is below 100%% that means you have spent more on Espionage than your opponent, and your missions may be performed at a discount. If the number is greater than 100%%, it means your opponent has spent more on Espionage than you. This will increase the cost of your Espionage Missions directed towards that opponent. The bottom item on the list states exactly how many Espionage Points you earn per turn towards that opponent. You can also see the Espionage Points you have against all players and the points they have against you by hovering the mouse over their score in the main interface.[PARAGRAPH:1]On the right half of the espionage screen is the list of all the Espionage Missions and their costs. You may click on an opponent's portrait to see a list of all their visible cities and the cost of missions in the selected city. Costs vary according to a number of factors, including the size of the city, its distance from your civilization, and religions present. If the cost of a Mission is blank, this means that the Mission requires more information to determine the exact cost, meaning you must move a spy into enemy territory before the mission cost can be determined. [PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spies[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Spies may be built by a Civilization that has researched [LINK=TECH_ALPHABET]The Alphabet[\LINK]. Spies differ greatly from most units. Completely invisible to enemy Civilizations, Spies can enter enemy territory to perform one Espionage Mission per turn. Active missions are tile sensitive, meaning you will have to sneak your Spy onto the enemy improvement or city you wish to affect. Performing a mission costs a certain number of Espionage Points and will cause an indicator to pop-up for your opponent over the location of the mission, informing him of the act (but not necessarily of your Spy). If the mission succeeds, the Spy may  survive to perform more Missions, or she may be caught and destroyed. If the mission fails, the Spy will be destroyed, but no Espionage Points will be consumed. The cost of these Espionage Missions decrease the longer a Spy remains stationary on a tile, but every turn a Spy remains in enemy territory, there is a chance that she will be caught and destroyed. The chances of being caught increase with your opponent's Espionage spending, with the presence of a Security Bureau and if your Spy has recently performed a mission against that tile. If the Spy is caught, there is also a chance that its owner will be revealed.[PARAGRAPH:1]In addition to performing Espionage Missions, Spies also make excellent intelligence gatherers. They can use enemy roads and enter the cities of opponents with whom the player is at war, allowing the player access to otherwise unreachable information. The downside is that spies have a chance to get caught when they end their turn in unwelcome territory, even if they perform no active missions. Unwelcome territory is any territory belonging to a rival with whom you do not have an open borders agreement.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Missions[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]To perform an Espionage Mission, a player must move a Spy unit to the plot or city where they would like to perform the mission and select the Espionage Mission button.[PARAGRAPH:1]The Espionage Missions are:[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabotage Improvement[\BOLD] - Destroy an enemy improvement or route[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabotage Building[\BOLD] - Destroy a building within an enemy city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Steal Treasury[\BOLD] - Steal a portion of your opponent's treasury, between 10 and 100%%[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Spread Culture[\BOLD] - Increase your cultural influence in a city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Poison Water[\BOLD] - Temporarily increase the amount of unhealthiness within an enemy city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Foment Unhappiness[\BOLD] - Temporarily increase unhappiness within an enemy city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Support City Revolt[\BOLD] - Send a city into revolt for one turn. This lowers enemy city defense, but does not remove cultural borders[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Steal Technology[\BOLD] - Steal a technology from an opponent[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabotage Production[\BOLD] - Destroys all hammers accumulated towards construction in a city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabotage Project[\BOLD] - Destroys an already completed Spaceship Part[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Influence Civics[\BOLD] - Switch one opponent's civic to match a civic you have adopted. AdvCiv change: Can also switch to any Economy or Religion civic except the two initial ones (top row).[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Influence Religion[\BOLD] - Switch an opponent's state religion to your state religion. [BOLD]Advanced Civ change[\BOLD]: Can also switch to any religion with nearly as many followers as the current state religion. [NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Perform Counterespionage[\BOLD] - Temporarily doubles cost of enemy espionage missions[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Passive Missions[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Passive Missions activate automatically as you accumulate certain levels of Espionage Points against an opponent. But as your opponent increases his own cache of Espionage Points, the Point costs of your Passive Missions increase in kind, requiring you to up your own Espionage spending to keep the effects. [PARAGRAPH:1]The Passive Missions are:[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Can See Demographics[\BOLD] - Allows you to see enemy demographics on the Info Screen [F9][NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Can See Research[\BOLD] - Allows you to see what your opponent is researching and how many turns they have until completion[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]City Visibility[\BOLD] - Enemy cities are added to your visibility[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Investigate City[\BOLD] - You may enter enemy city screens by double-clicking on them[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spy Specialists[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Spy specialists give a bonus to Espionage output and increase the chance of a Great Spy appearing in a city. The [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]Courthouse[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Jail[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]Intelligence Agency[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]Security Bureau[\LINK], and [LINK=BUILDING_KREMLIN]Kremlin[\LINK] World Wonder allow the assignment of Spy specialists in a city.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Great Spies[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Great Spies may occasionally appear in your city through the use of Spy specialists or Espionage-related Wonders. Invisible to all other units, Great Spies can explore rival territory, and will give a massive bonus of Espionage Points against a specific civilization if you expend the Spy in their city. They may also join a city as a super specialist, increasing science and espionage output of a city; or they may construct a [LINK=BUILDING_SCOTLAND_YARD]Scotland Yard[\LINK] in a city, adding a significant bonus to Espionage production in that city.</English>
+		<English>[H1]Espionage[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Espionage Points[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Using the Espionage Slider, players may assign a percentage of their trade income towards the production of Espionage Points. On the Espionage Screen, these points may be assigned to leaders that the player has met. These points may be used to perform various Active and Passive Espionage Missions. Active Missions require the player to spend Espionage Points and have a spy in the territory of the opponent they wish to perform the mission against. Passive Missions will take effect as the player accrues more points against a specific opponent. Having more espionage points accumulated will increase the cost of your opponents' missions towards you and reduce the cost of your missions towards them.[PARAGRAPH:1]The [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]Courthouse[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Jail[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_PALACE]Palace[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_CASTLE]Castle[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]Security Bureau[\LINK] and [LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]Intelligence Agency[\LINK] all increase the production of Espionage points. The [LINK=CIVIC_NATIONHOOD]Nationhood[\LINK] Civic also provides an Espionage bonus and the Security Bureau Building increases the point cost of enemy missions in your cities.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Espionage Screen[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Espionage Points may be assigned to specific opponents on the Espionage Screen. If you do not assign points to specific leaders, they will be evenly divided between all known civs. By increasing the "Weight" item next to each opponent's portrait, you can assign the portion you would like each opponent to receive. Increasing the weight next to an opponent's name to "1" will assign all Espionage Points to that opponent. Increasing one opponent's weight to "2" while keeping two other opponents' weight at "1" and another's at "0" will give the first opponent twice as many points as the second and third, while the fourth will receive none. You may assign Weights in any fractions you like, up to 99. In this way, you can finely tune the portion of Espionage Points you would like assigned to each of your opponents.[PARAGRAPH:1]Below "Weight" is a number that shows the cost modifier for your Espionage Missions against this opponent. This number is based on the total number of Espionage Points you have spent throughout the game against this opponent. If the number is below 100%% that means you have spent more on Espionage than your opponent, and your missions may be performed at a discount. If the number is greater than 100%%, it means your opponent has spent more on Espionage than you. This will increase the cost of your Espionage Missions directed towards that opponent. The bottom item on the list states exactly how many Espionage Points you earn per turn towards that opponent. You can also see the Espionage Points you have against all players and the points they have against you by hovering the mouse over their score in the main interface.[PARAGRAPH:1]On the right half of the espionage screen is the list of all the Espionage Missions and their costs. You may click on an opponent's portrait to see a list of all their visible cities and the cost of missions in the selected city. Costs vary according to a number of factors, including the size of the city, its distance from your civilization, and religions present. If the cost of a Mission is blank, this means that the Mission requires more information to determine the exact cost, meaning you must move a spy into enemy territory before the mission cost can be determined. [PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spies[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Spies may be built by a Civilization that has researched [LINK=TECH_ALPHABET]The Alphabet[\LINK]. Spies differ greatly from most units. Completely invisible to enemy Civilizations, Spies can enter enemy territory to perform one Espionage Mission per turn. Active missions are tile sensitive, meaning you will have to sneak your Spy onto the enemy improvement or city you wish to affect. Performing a mission costs a certain number of Espionage Points and will cause an indicator to pop-up for your opponent over the location of the mission, informing him of the act (but not necessarily of your Spy). If the mission succeeds, the Spy may  survive to perform more Missions, or she may be caught and destroyed. If the mission fails, the Spy will be destroyed, but no Espionage Points will be consumed. The cost of these Espionage Missions decrease the longer a Spy remains stationary on a tile, but every turn a Spy remains in enemy territory, there is a chance that she will be caught and destroyed. The chances of being caught increase with your opponent's Espionage spending, with the presence of a Security Bureau and if your Spy has recently performed a mission against that tile. If the Spy is caught, there is also a chance that its owner will be revealed.[PARAGRAPH:1]In addition to performing Espionage Missions, Spies also make excellent intelligence gatherers. They can use enemy roads and enter the cities of opponents with whom the player is at war, allowing the player access to otherwise unreachable information. The downside is that spies have a chance to get caught when they end their turn in unwelcome territory, even if they perform no active missions. Unwelcome territory is any territory belonging to a rival with whom you do not have an open borders agreement.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Missions[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]To perform an Espionage Mission, a player must move a Spy unit to the plot or city where they would like to perform the mission and select the Espionage Mission button.[PARAGRAPH:1]The Espionage Missions are:[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabotage Improvement[\BOLD] - Destroy an enemy improvement or route[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabotage Building[\BOLD] - Destroy a building within an enemy city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Steal Treasury[\BOLD] - Steal a portion of your opponent's treasury, between 10 and 100%%[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Spread Culture[\BOLD] - Increase your cultural influence in a city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Poison Water[\BOLD] - Temporarily increase the amount of unhealthiness within an enemy city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Foment Unhappiness[\BOLD] - Temporarily increase unhappiness within an enemy city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Support City Revolt[\BOLD] - Send a city into revolt for one turn. This lowers enemy city defense, but does not remove cultural borders[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Steal Technology[\BOLD] - Steal a technology from an opponent[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabotage Production[\BOLD] - Destroys all hammers accumulated towards construction in a city[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabotage Project[\BOLD] - Destroys an already completed Spaceship Part[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Influence Civics[\BOLD] - Switch one opponent's civic to match a civic you have adopted. [BOLD]AdvCiv change[\BOLD]: Can also switch to any Economy or Religion civic except the two initial ones (top row).[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Influence Religion[\BOLD] - Switch an opponent's state religion to your state religion. [BOLD]AdvCiv change[\BOLD]: Can also switch to any religion with nearly as many followers as the current state religion. [NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Perform Counterespionage[\BOLD] - Temporarily doubles cost of enemy espionage missions[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Passive Missions[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Passive Missions activate automatically as you accumulate certain levels of Espionage Points against an opponent. But as your opponent increases his own cache of Espionage Points, the Point costs of your Passive Missions increase in kind, requiring you to up your own Espionage spending to keep the effects. [PARAGRAPH:1]The Passive Missions are:[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Can See Demographics[\BOLD] - Allows you to see enemy demographics on the Info Screen [F9][NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Can See Research[\BOLD] - Allows you to see what your opponent is researching and how many turns they have until completion[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]City Visibility[\BOLD] - Enemy cities are added to your visibility[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Investigate City[\BOLD] - You may enter enemy city screens by double-clicking on them[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spy Specialists[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Spy specialists give a bonus to Espionage output and increase the chance of a Great Spy appearing in a city. The [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]Courthouse[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Jail[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]Intelligence Agency[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]Security Bureau[\LINK], and [LINK=BUILDING_KREMLIN]Kremlin[\LINK] World Wonder allow the assignment of Spy specialists in a city.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Great Spies[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Great Spies may occasionally appear in your city through the use of Spy specialists or Espionage-related Wonders. Invisible to all other units, Great Spies can explore rival territory, and will give a massive bonus of Espionage Points against a specific civilization if you expend the Spy in their city. They may also join a city as a super specialist, increasing science and espionage output of a city; or they may construct a [LINK=BUILDING_SCOTLAND_YARD]Scotland Yard[\LINK] in a city, adding a significant bonus to Espionage production in that city.</English>
 		<French>[H1]Espionnage[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Points d'espionnage[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Avec le curseur Espionnage, vous pouvez attribuer un pourcentage de vos revenus commerciaux &#224; la production de points d'espionnage. Ces points peuvent &#234;tre attribu&#233;s depuis l'&#233;cran d'espionnage aux dirigeants que vous avez rencontr&#233;. Ces points peuvent &#234;tre utilis&#233;s pour ex&#233;cuter diverses missions d'espionnage actives et passives. Lors des missions actives, vous devez d&#233;penser des points d'espionnage et avoir un espion sur le territoire de l'adversaire contre qui vous voulez ex&#233;cuter la mission. Les missions passives prennent effet alors que vous accumulez des points contre un adversaire sp&#233;cifique. Plus vous avez de points d'espionnage, plus les missions d'espionnage de votre adversaire contre vous seront co&#251;teuses, alors que vos missions contre lui seront moins ch&#232;res.[PARAGRAPH:1]Le [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]Tribunal[\LINK], la [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Prison[\LINK], le [LINK=BUILDING_PALACE]Palais[\LINK], le [LINK=BUILDING_CASTLE]Ch&#226;teau[\LINK], l'[LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]Agence de s&#233;curit&#233;[\LINK] et l'[LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]Agence de renseignement[\LINK] augmentent tous la production de points d'espionnage. L'[LINK=CIVIC_NATIONHOOD]Esprit national[\LINK] fournit aussi un bonus de points d'espionnage et le b&#226;timent de l'Agence de s&#233;curit&#233; augmente le co&#251;t en points des missions ennemies dans vos villes.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Ecran d'espionnage[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Les points d'espionnage peuvent &#234;tre attribu&#233;s &#224; des adversaires sp&#233;cifiques gr&#226;ce &#224; l'&#233;cran d'espionnage. Si vous n'attribuez pas de points &#224; des dirigeants sp&#233;cifiques, ces points seront &#233;quitablement r&#233;partis entre les civilisations connues. En augmentant l'&#233;l&#233;ment Importance &#224; c&#244;t&#233; du portrait de chaque adversaire, vous augmentez le nombre de points d'espionnage que vous lui attribuez. Si vous choisissez de mettre 1 point &#224; un adversaire et 0 &#224; un autre, tous vos points d'espionnage iront &#224; chaque tour sur le premier adversaire. Si vous attribuez 2 points &#224; un adversaire, 1 point &#224; deux autres et 0 au dernier, le premier adversaire utilisera deux fois plus de points d'espionnage que les deux suivants, et le dernier n'en aura aucun. Vous pouvez assigner l'Importance comme bon vous semble, jusqu'&#224; 99. Vous pouvez ainsi ajuster pr&#233;cis&#233;ment votre r&#233;partition des points d'espionnage.[PARAGRAPH:1]Sous l'Importance se trouve un nombre indiquant le modificateur du co&#251;t de vos missions d'espionnage contre cet adversaire. Ce nombre est bas&#233; sur les points que vous avez investis contre cet adversaire durant la partie. Si le pourcentage est inf&#233;rieur &#224; 100 %, vous avez investi plus de points d'espionnage contre cet adversaire que lui n'en a investis contre vous. Vos missions sont donc rentables. Si le pourcentage est sup&#233;rieur &#224; 100 %, c'est le contraire, ce qui rend les missions d'espionnage contre cet adversaire plus co&#251;teuses. L'&#233;l&#233;ment en bas de la liste &#233;tablit le nombre exact de points d'espionnage que vous gagnez par tour contre cet adversaire. Vous pouvez aussi voir combien de points d'espionnage vous avez contre chaque adversaire, ainsi que ceux qu'ils ont contre vous, en pla&#231;ant le curseur de la souris sur leur score dans l'&#233;cran principal.[PARAGRAPH:1]Le panneau droit de l'&#233;cran affiche toutes les missions d'espionnage et leur co&#251;t. Cliquez sur le portrait de l'adversaire contre lequel vous voulez ex&#233;cuter une mission pour faire appara&#238;tre la liste de ses villes visibles. Cliquez sur une ville pour conna&#238;tre le co&#251;t d'une mission contre celle-ci. Le co&#251;t des missions varie en fonction de la taille de la ville, de la distance qui la s&#233;pare de votre civilisation et des religions repr&#233;sent&#233;es. Si le co&#251;t d'une mission n'est pas afich&#233;, la mission n&#233;cessite plus d'informations. En g&#233;n&#233;ral, il faut qu'un espion soit en territoire ennemi avant de pouvoir d&#233;terminer le co&#251;t de la mission. [PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Espions[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Les espions peuvent &#234;tre produits par une civilisation qui poss&#232;de l'[LINK=TECH_ALPHABET]Alphabet[\LINK]. Les espions sont tr&#232;s diff&#233;rents de la plupart des autres unit&#233;s. Les espions sont invisibles aux yeux des autres civilisations et peuvent p&#233;n&#233;trer en territoire ennemi pour ex&#233;cuter une mission d'espionnage par tour. Les missions actives concernent les cases. Vous devez donc infiltrer votre espion dans la ville ou la zone ennemie vis&#233;e. Ex&#233;cuter une mission co&#251;te des points d'espionnage et sera signal&#233; &#224; votre adversaire par un indicateur. Celui-ci montrera l'emplacement de la mission (mais pas n&#233;cessairement votre espion). Si la mission r&#233;ussit, l'espion peut survivre et ex&#233;cuter d'autres missions. Il peut aussi &#234;tre tu&#233;. Si la mission &#233;choue, l'espion est d&#233;truit, mais cela ne co&#251;tera pas de points d'espionnage. Plus un espion reste sur une case, moins la mission d'espionnage contre cette case est co&#251;teuse mais, &#224; chaque tour, il peut &#234;tre attrap&#233; et d&#233;truit. Les chances d'&#234;tre pris augmente avec les d&#233;penses en espionnage de votre adversaire, avec la pr&#233;sence d'un Bureau de s&#233;curit&#233; et du fait que votre espion ait ex&#233;cut&#233; r&#233;cemment une mission sur la case vis&#233;e. Si l'espion est pris, il y a aussi une chance que son propri&#233;taire soit r&#233;v&#233;l&#233;..[PARAGRAPH:1]En plus d'ex&#233;cuter des missions d'espionnage, les espions font d'excellents collecteurs d'informations. Ils peuvent utiliser les routes ennemies et entrer dans les villes de adversaires en guerre avec vous, vous permettant d'obtenir des informations inaccessibles. Le revers de la m&#233;daille est que les espions ont des chances d'&#234;tre pris lorsqu'ils finissent leur tour en territoire ennemi, m&#234;me s'ils n'ex&#233;cutaient pas de missions actives. Les territoires ennemis englobent les territoires d'un adversaire avec qui vous n'avez pas d'accord de libre passage.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Missions[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Pour ex&#233;cuter une mission d'espionnage, vous devez d&#233;placer l'espion sur la case ou la ville cibl&#233;e et appuyer sur le bouton de s&#233;lection des missions d'espionnage.[PARAGRAPH:1]Les missions d'espionnage sont :[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Saboter l'am&#233;nagement[\BOLD] - d&#233;truit une am&#233;lioration ou une route ennemie[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Saboter le b&#226;timent[\BOLD] - d&#233;truit un b&#226;timent dans une ville ennemie[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Voler le tr&#233;sor[\BOLD] - vole entre 10 et 100 % du tr&#233;sor de votre adversaire[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Propager la culture[\BOLD] - augmente votre niveau de culture dans une ville[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Empoisonner l'eau[\BOLD] - augmente temporairement l'insalubrit&#233; d'une ville ennemie[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Susciter le m&#233;contentement[\BOLD] - augmente temporairement le m&#233;contentement dans une ville ennemie[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Soutenir la r&#233;volte de la ville[\BOLD] - plonge une ville dans la r&#233;volte pendant un tour. Cela r&#233;duit les d&#233;fenses de l'ennemi, mais pas ses fronti&#232;res[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Voler une technologie[\BOLD] - vole une technologie d'un adversaire[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Saboter la production[\BOLD] - d&#233;truit tous les marteaux accumul&#233;s pour la construction dans une ville[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Saboter le projet[\BOLD] - d&#233;truit toutes les parties d&#233;j&#224; termin&#233;es d'un vaisseau spatial[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Influencer la doctrine[\BOLD] - l'adversaire adopte l'une de vos doctrines[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Influencer la religion [\BOLD] - l'adversaire adopte votre religion d'&#233;tat[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Effectuer une mission de contre-espionnage[\BOLD] - double temporairement le co&#251;t des missions d'espionnage ennemies[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Missions passives[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Les missions passives peuvent &#234;tre ex&#233;cut&#233;es automatiquement en accumulant contre un adversaire le nombre de points d'espionnage n&#233;cessaire. Le niveau requis pour ex&#233;cuter ces missions augmente avec les points d'espionnage de vos ennemis. [PARAGRAPH:1]Les missions passives sont :[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Peut voir la d&#233;mographie d'une ville[\BOLD] - vous permet de voir la d&#233;mographie d'une ville ennemie sur l'&#233;cran d'informations [F9][NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Peut voir l'&#233;tat des recherches[\BOLD] - vous permet de voir la recherche effectu&#233;e par votre adversaire et le nombre de tours avant qu'il ne termine cette recherche[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Afficher la ville[\BOLD] - la ville ennemie est ajout&#233;e &#224; votre champ de vision[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Enqu&#234;ter sur la ville[\BOLD] - vous permet de voir l'&#233;cran d'une ville ennemie en double-cliquant sur cette ville[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Espions sp&#233;cialistes[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Les Espions sp&#233;cialistes donnent un bonus d'espionnage,  et augmentent les chances qu'un Espion illustre apparaisse dans une ville. Le [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]Tribunal[\LINK], la [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Prison[\LINK], le [LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]Service de renseignements[\LINK], le [LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]Bureau de s&#233;curit&#233;[\LINK], et le [LINK=BUILDING_KREMLIN]Kremlin[\LINK] permettent d'assigner une Espion sp&#233;cialiste dans une ville.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Espions illustres[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Les chances qu'un Espion illustre apparaisse d&#233;pendent des b&#226;timents d'une ville et du nombre d'Espions sp&#233;cialistes pr&#233;sents. Les Espions illustres sont invisibles aux yeux des autres unit&#233;s, y compris les Espions et les Espions illustres ennemis. Les Espions illustres peuvent explorer les territoires ennemis. Ils vous donnent un bonus de points d'espionnage tr&#232;s important contre une civilisation si vous en placez un dans l'une de leurs villes. Les Espions illustres peuvent aussi rejoindre une ville en tant que "super sp&#233;cialistes", ce qui augmente la Science et l'Espionnage de la ville. Ils peuvent aussi construire la merveille nationale [LINK=BUILDING_SCOTLAND_YARD]Scotland Yard[\LINK], ce qui ajoute un bonus important &#224; la production d'espionnage de cette ville.</French>
 		<German>[H1]Spionage[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spionagepunkte[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Mit dem Spionageregler k&#246;nnen Spieler der Produktion von Spionagepunkten einen Prozentanteil ihres Handelseinkommens zuweisen. Auf dem Spionagebildschirm k&#246;nnen diese Punkte den Staatsoberh&#228;uptern zugewiesen werden, die der Spieler bereits getroffen hat. Mit den Punkten k&#246;nnen zahlreiche aktive und passive Spionagemissionen durchgef&#252;hrt werden. Bei aktiven Missionen muss der Spieler Spionagepunkte ausgeben und schickt einen Spion in das Gebiet des Gegners, der Ziel der Mission ist. Passive Missionen beginnen, wenn der Spieler eine gewisse Anzahl von Punkten gegen einen speziellen Gegner gesammelt hat. Je mehr Spionagepunkte Sie gesammelt haben, umso teurer werden die feindlichen Missionen gegen Ihre Zivilisation und umso billiger werden Ihre Missionen gegen Ihre Gegner.[PARAGRAPH:1]Die Geb&#228;ude [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Gef&#228;ngnis[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]Gerichtsgeb&#228;ude[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_PALACE]Palast[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_CASTLE]Schloss[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]Sicherheitsdienst[\LINK] und [LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]Geheimdienst[\LINK] erh&#246;hen alle die Produktion von Spionagepunkten. Au&#223;erdem gew&#228;hrt die Staatsform [LINK=CIVIC_NATIONHOOD]Nationale Selbstst&#228;ndigkeit[\LINK] einen Spionagebonus und der Sicherheitsdienst erh&#246;ht die Punktekosten feindlicher Missionen in Ihren St&#228;dten.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spionagebildschirm[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Spionagepunkte k&#246;nnen im Spionagebildschirm speziellen Gegnern zugewiesen werden. Wenn Sie Punkte nicht speziellen Staatsoberh&#228;uptern zuweisen, werden sie gleichm&#228;&#223;ig auf alle bekannten Zivilisationen aufgeteilt. Sie k&#246;nnen einem Gegner den gew&#252;nschten Anteil zuweisen, indem Sie die "Gewichtung" neben dem Portr&#228;t des Gegners anpassen. Wenn Sie die Gewichtung neben dem gegnerischen Namen auf "1" erh&#246;hen, werden alle Spionagepunkte diesem Gegner zugewiesen. Wenn Sie die Gewichtung eines Gegner auf "2" erh&#246;hen, w&#228;hrend zwei andere Gegner eine Gewichtung von "1" haben und ein weiterer Gegner "0" hat, erh&#228;lt der erste Gegner doppelt so viele Punkte wie der zweite und dritte, w&#228;hrend der vierte Gegner keine Punkte erh&#228;lt. Sie k&#246;nnen Gewichtung beliebig aufteilen, bis zu 99. So ist es m&#246;glich, den Anteil der Spionagepunkte, die Sie den einzelnen Gegnern zuweisen m&#252;ssen, sehr genau festzulegen.[PARAGRAPH:1]Unter "Gewichtung" wird eine Zahl angezeigt, die den Kostenmodifikator f&#252;r Spionagemissionen gegen diesen Gegner angibt. Diese Zahl basiert auf den gesamten Spionagepunkten, die Sie im Laufe des Spiels gegen diesen Gegner ausgegeben haben. Wenn sie unter 100%% liegt, bedeutet das, Sie haben mehr Spionagepunkte ausgegeben als Ihr Gegner, und Ihre Missionen gegen ihn sind billiger. Wenn die Zahl &#252;ber 100%% liegt, bedeutet das, Ihr Gegner hat mehr f&#252;r Spionage gegen Sie ausgegeben. Der unterste Wert auf der Liste gibt genau an, wie viele Spionagepunkte Sie pro Runde gegen diesen Gegner erhalten. Sie k&#246;nnen auch Ihre Spionagepunkte gegen&#252;ber allen Spielern sehen bzw. die Ihrer Gegner Ihnen gegen&#252;ber, indem Sie den Mauszeiger &#252;ber den Punktestand im Hauptbildschirm bewegen.[PARAGRAPH:1]Rechts im Spionagebildschirm befindet sich eine Liste aller Spionagemissionen und ihrer jeweiligen Kosten. Wenn Sie auf das Portr&#228;t eines Gegner klicken, sehen Sie eine Liste aller ihrer sichtbaren St&#228;dte und die Kosten von Missionen in der ausgew&#228;hlten Stadt. Die Kosten variieren aufgrund unterschiedlicher Faktoren, u. A. Gr&#246;&#223;e der Stadt, Entfernung von Ihrer Zivilisation und vorhandene Religionen. Wenn bei einer Mission keine Kosten angegeben sind, bedeutet das, f&#252;r die Mission sind weitere Informationen erforderlich, um die genauen Kosten zu bestimmen, d. h. Sie m&#252;ssen einen Spion in das feindliche Gebiet schicken, bevor die Missionskosten festgelegt werden k&#246;nnen.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spione[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Spione k&#246;nnen von einer Zivilisation errichtet werden, die das [LINK=TECH_ALPHABET]Alphabet[\LINK] erforscht hat. Spione unterschieden sich grundlegend von den meisten Einheiten. Sie sind f&#252;r feindliche Zivilisationen vollkommen unsichtbar und k&#246;nnen feindliches Gebiet betreten, um eine Spionagemission pro Runde durchzuf&#252;hren. Aktive Missionen sind abh&#228;ngig vom Gel&#228;ndefeld, d. h. Sie m&#252;ssen Ihren Spion zu der entsprechenden feindlichen Modernisierung oder Stadt schicken, die Sie ausspionieren wollen. Die Durchf&#252;hrung einer Mission kostet einen bestimmten Betrag an Spionagepunkten und Ihr Gegner erh&#228;lt &#252;ber dem Ziel der Mission einen Hinweis, der ihn &#252;ber den Vorgang informiert (aber nicht notwendigerweise &#252;ber Ihren Spion). Wenn die Mission erfolgreich ist, &#252;berlebt der Spion entweder und kann weitere Missionen ausf&#252;hren, oder er wird gefasst und zerst&#246;rt. Wenn die Mission scheitert, wird der Spion zerst&#246;rt, aber es werden keine Spionagepunkte verbraucht. Die Kosten dieser Spionagemissionen sinken, je l&#228;nger ein Spion auf einem Gel&#228;ndefeld stationiert bleibt, aber pro Runde, die sich ein Spion auf feindlichem Gebiet befindet, besteht die M&#246;glichkeit, dass er gefasst und zerst&#246;rt wird. Die Chance, entdeckt zu werden, steigt mit den Ausgaben des Gegners f&#252;r Spionage und der Errichtung eines Sicherheitsdienstes. Hoch ist sie auch, knapp nachdem der Spion eine Mission auf Ihrem Gel&#228;ndefeld durchgef&#252;hrt hat. Wenn der Spion gefasst wird, kann es auch sein, dass sein Auftraggeber preisgegeben wird.[PARAGRAPH:1]Au&#223;er zur Durchf&#252;hrung von Spionagemissionen eigenen sich Spione auch hervorragend zum Sammeln von Geheiminformationen. Sie k&#246;nnen sich auf den gegnerischen Stra&#223;en fortbewegen und St&#228;dte von Gegnern betreten, mit denen sich der Spieler im Krieg befindet, wodurch dieser an sonst unerreichbare Informationen gelangt. Der Nachteil ist, dass Spione gefasst werden k&#246;nnen, wenn sie ihre Runde auf feindlichem Gebiet beenden, auch wenn sie gerade keine aktiven Missionen durchf&#252;hren. Feindliches Gebiet ist in diesem Fall jedes Gebiet, das einem Gegner geh&#246;rt, mit dem Sie kein Offene-Grenzen-Abkommen haben.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Missionen[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Um eine Spionagemission durchzuf&#252;hren, muss ein Spieler eine Spionageeinheit auf das jeweilige Gel&#228;ndefeld bzw. zur jeweiligen Stadt bewegen und den Button 'Spionagemission' ausw&#228;hlen.[PARAGRAPH:1]Zu den Spionagemissionen geh&#246;ren:[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Modernisierung sabotieren[\BOLD] - Zerst&#246;ren Sie eine Modernisierung oder einen Weg des Gegners[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Geb&#228;ude sabotieren[\BOLD] - Zerst&#246;ren Sie ein Geb&#228;ude innerhalb einer feindlichen Stadt[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Schatzkammer ausrauben[\BOLD] - Stehlen Sie einen Teil des gegnerischen Verm&#246;gens, zwischen 10%% und 100%%[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Kultur verbreiten[\BOLD] - Erh&#246;hen Sie Ihren kulturellen Einfluss in einer Stadt[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Wasser vergiften[\BOLD] - Senken Sie vor&#252;bergehend den Gesundheitswert einer feindlichen Stadt[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Unzufriedenheit sch&#252;ren[\BOLD] - Steigern Sie vor&#252;bergehend die Unzufriedenheit in einer feindlichen Stadt[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Stadt-Revolte unterst&#252;tzen[\BOLD] - L&#246;st eine Runde lang eine Revolte in einer Stadt aus. Dadurch wird die feindliche Stadtverteidigung reduziert, aber kulturelle Grenzen werden nicht beseitigt.[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Technologie stehlen[\BOLD] - Stehlen Sie einem Gegner Technologie[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Produktion sabotieren[\BOLD] - Zerst&#246;rt alle H&#228;mmer, die f&#252;r Bauvorhaben in einer Stadt dienen[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Projekt sabotieren[\BOLD] - Zerst&#246;rt ein bereits fertig gestelltes Raumschiffteil[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Staatsform beeinflussen[\BOLD] - &#196;ndern Sie die Staatsform eines Gegners, um sie Ihrer eigenen Staatsform anzupassen. AdvCiv-�nderung: Man kann jede �konomie- und Religionsform ausw�hlen au�er denen in der ersten Zeile des Staatsformen-Bildschirms.[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Religion beeinflussen[\BOLD] - &#196;ndern Sie die Staatsreligion eines Gegners zu Ihrer eigenen Staatsreligion[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Spionageabwehr ausf&#252;hren[\BOLD] - Verdoppeln Sie vor&#252;bergehend die Kosten feindlicher Spionagemissionen[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Passive Missionen[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Passive Missionen werden automatisch aktiviert, sobald Sie eine bestimmte Anzahl an Spionagepunkten gegen einen Gegner gesammelt haben. Wenn Ihr Gegner seine Spionagepunkte steigert, erh&#246;hen sich dementsprechend die Punktekosten Ihrer passiven Missionen. Um also dieselbe Wirkung zu erzielen, m&#252;ssen Sie Ihre eigenen Spionageausgaben erh&#246;hen.[PARAGRAPH:1]Zu den passiven Missionen geh&#246;ren:[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Demografie sichtbar[\BOLD] - Sie k&#246;nnen die feindliche Demografie auf dem Infobildschirm [F9] sehen.[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Forschung sichtbar[\BOLD] - Sie sehen, was Ihr Gegner erforscht und wie viele Runden noch zur Fertigstellung verbleiben.[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Stadtsichtbarkeit[\BOLD] - Feindliche St&#228;dte werden zu Ihrem Blickfeld hinzugef&#252;gt[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Stadt erkunden[\BOLD] - Sie k&#246;nnen die Bildschirme gegnerischer St&#228;dte aufrufen, indem Sie auf die jeweilige Stadt doppelklicken.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spion-Spezialisten[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Spion-Spezialisten gew&#228;hren einen Bonus auf die Spionageproduktion und erh&#246;hen die Chance, dass ein Gro&#223;er Spion in einer Stadt auftaucht. Mit den Geb&#228;uden [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Gef&#228;ngnis[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]Gerichtsgeb&#228;ude[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]Geheimdienst[\LINK], [LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]Sicherheitsdienst[\LINK] und dem Weltwunder [LINK=BUILDING_KREMLIN]Kreml[\LINK] k&#246;nnen Sie einer Stadt einen Spion-Spezialisten zuweisen.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Gro&#223;e Spione[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Gro&#223;e Spione k&#246;nnen gelegentlich in Ihrer Stadt auftauchen, wenn sie Spion-Spezialisten oder f&#252;r Spionage relevante Wunder errichten. Sie sind f&#252;r alle anderen Einheiten unsichtbar und k&#246;nnen so gegnerische Gebiete erkunden. Sie gew&#228;hren einen riesigen Bonus auf Spionagepunkte gegen eine spezielle Zivilisation, wenn Sie den Spion in deren Stadt einsetzen. Sie k&#246;nnen eine Stadt auch als Superspezialist unterst&#252;tzen, wodurch die Wissenschafts- und Spionageproduktion der Stadt gesteigert wird; oder Sie k&#246;nnen in einer Stadt den [LINK=BUILDING_SCOTLAND_YARD]Scotland Yard[\LINK] errichten, was dieser Stadt einen deutlichen Bonus auf Spionageproduktion gew&#228;hrt.</German>
 		<Italian>[H1]Spionaggio[\H1][PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Punti spionaggio[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Usando la barra dello spionaggio, i giocatori possono assegnare una percentuale dei ricavi da commercio alla produzione di punti spionaggio. Nella schermata dello spionaggio questi punti possono essere assegnati ai vari leader incontrati. I punti possono essere usati per compiere varie missioni di spionaggio, attive e passive. Per le missioni attive &#232; necessario che il giocatore spenda punti spionaggio e abbia una spia nel territorio del nemico vittima della missione. Le missioni passive avranno effetto man mano che il giocatore accumula punti contro uno specifico avversario. Con l'accumulo dei punti spionaggio, da un lato vengono ridotti i costi delle missioni nei confronti di quell'avversario, dall'altro vengono aumentati i costi delle missioni dell'avversario nei confronti del giocatore.[PARAGRAPH:1]Il [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]palazzo di giustizia[\LINK], la [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]prigione[\LINK], il [LINK=BUILDING_PALACE]palazzo[\LINK], il [LINK=BUILDING_CASTLE]castello[\LINK], l'[LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]agenzia di sicurezza[\LINK] e i [LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]servizi segreti[\LINK] incrementano la produzione di punti spionaggio. La forma di governo [LINK=CIVIC_NATIONHOOD]Identit&#224; nazionale[\LINK] produce un bonus di spionaggio e l'edificio dell'agenzia di sicurezza aumenta il costo in punti delle missioni nemiche nelle tue citt&#224;.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Schermata dello spionaggio[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]I punti spionaggio possono essere assegnati contro avversari specifici nella schermata dello spionaggio. Se ci&#242; non avviene, essi verranno suddivisi equamente tra tutte le civilt&#224; note. Aumentando il "Peso" accanto al ritratto di un avversario, puoi regolare l'attenzione da dedicare a ciascuno. Portando il peso di un leader a "1", tutti i punti spionaggio verranno assegnati a lui. Portando il peso di un leader a "2", mentre il peso di altri due &#232; a "1" e quello di un altro ancora a "0", assegnerai al primo il doppio dei punti rispetto al secondo e al terzo, mentre al quarto non ne verr&#224; assegnato nessuno. Il peso &#232; assegnabile in frazioni da 0 a 99. In questo modo puoi ottimizzare l'allocazione dei punti spionaggio nel modo che preferisci.[PARAGRAPH:1]Sotto il "Peso" c'&#232; un numero: esso rappresenta il modificatore dei costi di missioni di spionaggio nei confronti di quell'avversario. Il numero &#232; calcolato sulla base di tutti i punti spionaggio spesi contro quell'avversario durante la partita. Se il numero &#232; inferiore al 100%%, questo significa che in spionaggio hai speso pi&#249; punti del tuo avversario e che le tue missioni costeranno meno. Se invece &#232; superiore al 100%%, &#232; vero il contrario e le tue missioni contro quell'avversario costeranno di pi&#249;. L'elemento in fondo all'elenco mostra esattamente quanti punti spionaggio accumuli per turno contro quell'avversario. Puoi scoprire i punti spionaggio accumulati contro tutti i giocatori e i punti che loro hanno accumulato nei tuoi confronti puntando il mouse sopra i loro punteggi nell'interfaccia principale.[PARAGRAPH:1]Nella parte destra della schermata dello spionaggio si trova l'elenco delle missioni con il relativo costo. Clicca sul ritratto di un leader per vedere l'elenco delle sue citt&#224; visibili e i costi delle missioni nella citt&#224; selezionata. I costi dipendono da una serie di fattori, tra cui le dimensioni della citt&#224;, la sua distanza dai tuoi confini e la religione. Se il costo non &#232; indicato, significa che per determinarlo servono ulteriori informazioni: dovrai portare una spia nel territorio nemico per valutare la situazione. [PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spie[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Le spie possono essere prodotte dalle civilt&#224; che hanno ricercato l'[LINK=TECH_ALPHABET]Alfabeto[\LINK]. Le spie sono molto diverse dalla gran parte delle unit&#224;. Completamente invisibili alle civilt&#224; nemiche, possono entrare in territorio nemico per compiere una missione di spionaggio per turno. Le missioni attive possono essere eseguite solo se la spia si trova sulla casella del miglioramento o della citt&#224; da sabotare. Ogni missione ha un costo in punti spionaggio (all'avversario apparir&#224; un messaggio che lo informer&#224; del sabotaggio, ma non necessariamente del responsabile). Se la missione &#232; completata con successo, la spia pu&#242; sopravvivere per essere usata in seguito, oppure essere catturata e distrutta. Se la missione fallisce, la spia &#232; distrutta, ma non verranno consumati punti spionaggio. Il costo di una missione diminuisce quanto pi&#249; la spia rimane ferma nella casella, ma ogni turno che la spia rimane ferma c'&#232; la possibilit&#224; che venga catturata e distrutta. Le probabilit&#224; di distruzione aumentano con la spesa in spionaggio da parte dell'avversario, con la presenza di un'agenzia di sicurezza e se la spia ha compiuto di recente una missione in quella casella. Se la spia viene catturata, c'&#232; anche la possibilit&#224; che venga rivelato il mandante.[PARAGRAPH:1]Oltre a compiere missioni di spionaggio, le spie sono anche ottime fonti di informazioni. Esse possono usare le strade nemiche ed entrare nelle citt&#224; dei nemici con cui il giocatore &#232; in guerra, fornendo a quest'ultimo informazioni che altrimenti ignorerebbe. Lo svantaggio &#232; che a ogni fine turno in territorio nemico (ossia di un nemico con cui non esiste un accordo di diritto di passaggio) esiste la possibilit&#224; che le spie vengano catturate, anche se non hanno compiuto missioni attive.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Missioni[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Per compiere una missione di spionaggio, il giocatore deve spostare la spia nella casella o citt&#224; oggetto della missione e selezionare il pulsante Missione di spionaggio.[PARAGRAPH:1]Le missioni di spionaggio sono:[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabota miglioramenti[\BOLD] - Distruggi un miglioramento o una rotta nemici[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabota edifici[\BOLD] - Distruggi un edificio in una citt&#224; nemica[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Ruba tesoro[\BOLD] - Ruba parte del tesoro di un nemico, tra il 10 e il 100%%[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Diffondi cultura[\BOLD] - Aumenta la tua influenza culturale in una citt&#224;[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Avvelena acqua[\BOLD] - Aumenta temporaneamente la malasanit&#224; in una citt&#224; nemica[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Alimenta infelicit&#224;[\BOLD] - Aumenta temporaneamente l'infelicit&#224; in una citt&#224; nemica[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Supporta rivolta cittadina[\BOLD] - Fai rivoltare una citt&#224; per un turno, abbassando le difese cittadine (non rimuove i confini culturali)[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Ruba tecnologie[\BOLD] - Ruba una tecnologia a un nemico[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabota produzione[\BOLD] - Distrugge tutti gli scudi accumulati per la costruzione in una citt&#224;[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Sabota progetto[\BOLD] - Distrugge una componente dell'astronave spaziale gi&#224; costruita[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Influenza forme di governo[\BOLD] - Cambia la forma di governo di un nemico, facendogliene adottare una da te gi&#224; adottata[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Influenza religione[\BOLD] - La tua religione di stato diventa anche la religione di stato del nemico[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Effettua controspionaggio[\BOLD] - Raddoppia temporaneamente i costi delle missioni di spionaggio nemiche[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Missioni passive[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Le missioni passive si attivano automaticamente al raggiungimento di determinate soglie di punti spionaggio contro un avversario. Ma man mano che anche l'avversario aumenta il proprio tesoro di punti spionaggio contro di te, il costo delle missioni passive aumenta corrispondentemente, rendendo necessaria una maggior spesa per mantenere in atto la missione passiva. [PARAGRAPH:1]Le missioni passive sono:[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Visiona demografica[\BOLD] - Permette di visionare la demografia nemica nella schermata di informazione [F9][NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Visiona ricerche[\BOLD] - Permette di visionare le ricerche in corso del nemico e di sapere quanti turni mancano al completamento[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Visibilit&#224; citt&#224;[\BOLD] - Le citt&#224; nemiche sono visibili[NEWLINE][TAB][BOLD]Infiltrati nella citt&#224;[\BOLD] - Puoi accedere alle schermate cittadine del nemico cliccando due volte sulle citt&#224;[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Spie specialiste[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Le Spie specialiste forniscono un bonus allo spionaggio e aumentano le possibilit&#224; di far apparire una Grande Spia nelle citt&#224;. Il [LINK=BUILDING_COURTHOUSE]Palazzo di giustizia[\LINK], la [LINK=BUILDING_JAIL]Prigione[\LINK], i [LINK=BUILDING_INTELLIGENCE_AGENCY]Servizi segreti[\LINK], l'[LINK=BUILDING_NATIONAL_SECURITY]Agenzia di sicurezza[\LINK] e la Grande Meraviglia [LINK=BUILDING_KREMLIN]Cremlino[\LINK] permettono l'assegnazione di una Spia specialista in una citt&#224;.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Grandi Spie[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Le Grandi Spie possono apparire di quando in quando in una delle tue citt&#224;, mediante l'uso di Spie specialiste oppure grazie alle Meraviglie correlate allo spionaggio. Invisibili a tutte le altre unit&#224;, le Grandi Spie possono esplorare il territorio nemico oppure ottenere dei grandi bonus di punti spionaggio se si sacrifica l'unit&#224; in una citt&#224; di un'altra civilt&#224;. Inoltre, le Grandi Spie possono anche unirsi a una citt&#224; nelle vesti di super specialisti, aumentando lo spionaggio e la produzione scientifica di una citt&#224; oppure realizzare [LINK=BUILDING_SCOTLAND_YARD]Scotland Yard[\LINK] in una citt&#224;, aumentando significativamente lo spionaggio di quella citt&#224;.</Italian>
